The table structure information is (table names): 
['actor', 'country', 'city', 'address', 'language', 'category', 'customer', 'film', 'film_actor', 'film_category', 'film_text', 'inventory', 'staff', 'store', 'payment', 'rental']
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns for the required analysis  
(actor‐centred “top‑3 revenue films” and per‑actor share):

actor.actor_id: joins actor to film_actor to identify each performer  
actor.first_name / actor.last_name: display actor’s full name in reports  
film_actor.film_id: links an actor to every film he / she appears in  
film_actor.actor_id: same junction table, needed for counting actors per film  
film.film_id: primary key, used to aggregate revenue to the film level  
film.title: film name for readability  
inventory.film_id: ties each physical copy to its film  
inventory.inventory_id: key that connects to rental  
rental.inventory_id: relates rentals to the copy (and therefore the film)  
rental.rental_id: key that connects to payment  
payment.rental_id: connects money to a rental  
payment.amount: dollar amount that contributes to a film’s total revenue  

Typical join / filter conditions that will be used later  
• film_actor.film_id = film.film_id  
• inventory.film_id  = film.film_id  
• rental.inventory_id = inventory.inventory_id  
• payment.rental_id   = rental.rental_id  
• payment.amount IS NOT NULL (optional quality filter)  

Below are ten exploratory Sqlite queries (each limited to 20 rows) that gradually
build understanding from simple listings to the final “top‑3 revenue films per
actor” result.

```sql
-- 1) List a sample of actors
-- Description: basic look at performer identifiers and names.
SELECT DISTINCT
       "actor_id",
       "first_name",
       "last_name"
FROM   "actor"
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 2) List a sample of films
-- Description: confirm film ids and titles.
SELECT DISTINCT
       "film_id",
       "title"
FROM   "film"
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 3) What films does actor #1 appear in?
-- Description: actor–film relationship check via junction table.
SELECT DISTINCT
       fa."film_id"
FROM   "film_actor" AS fa
WHERE  fa."actor_id" = 1
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Inventory copies for film #1
-- Description: see how many physical copies tie to a single film.
SELECT DISTINCT
       "inventory_id",
       "film_id",
       "store_id"
FROM   "inventory"
WHERE  "film_id" = 1
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Rentals of those inventory copies
-- Description: connect inventory to rental activity.
SELECT DISTINCT
       r."rental_id",
       r."inventory_id",
       r."customer_id",
       r."rental_date"
FROM   "rental"  AS r
WHERE  r."inventory_id" IN (SELECT "inventory_id"
                            FROM   "inventory"
                            WHERE  "film_id" = 1)
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Payments that belong to those rentals
-- Description: validate that money flows from rentals to payments.
SELECT DISTINCT
       p."payment_id",
       p."rental_id",
       p."amount"
FROM   "payment" AS p
WHERE  p."rental_id" IN (SELECT "rental_id"
                         FROM   "rental"
                         WHERE  "inventory_id" IN (SELECT "inventory_id"
                                                   FROM   "inventory"
                                                   WHERE  "film_id" = 1))
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Total revenue per film (all films)
-- Description: aggregate money earned by every film.
SELECT
       f."film_id",
       f."title",
       SUM(p."amount") AS "total_revenue"
FROM   "film"        AS f
JOIN   "inventory"   AS i ON i."film_id"   = f."film_id"
JOIN   "rental"      AS r ON r."inventory_id" = i."inventory_id"
JOIN   "payment"     AS p ON p."rental_id"    = r."rental_id"
GROUP  BY f."film_id"
ORDER  BY "total_revenue" DESC
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Count of actors per film
-- Description: will be used to compute equal revenue shares.
SELECT
       fa."film_id",
       COUNT(DISTINCT fa."actor_id") AS "actor_count"
FROM   "film_actor" AS fa
GROUP  BY fa."film_id"
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Per‑actor revenue share in each film
-- Description: divide total film revenue by number of actors.
SELECT
       fa."actor_id",
       fa."film_id",
       SUM(p."amount") / COUNT(DISTINCT fa2."actor_id") AS "actor_revenue"
FROM   "film_actor"  AS fa
JOIN   "film_actor"  AS fa2 ON fa2."film_id" = fa."film_id"
JOIN   "inventory"   AS i   ON i."film_id"   = fa."film_id"
JOIN   "rental"      AS r   ON r."inventory_id" = i."inventory_id"
JOIN   "payment"     AS p   ON p."rental_id"    = r."rental_id"
GROUP  BY fa."actor_id", fa."film_id"
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
--10) Top‑3 revenue‑generating films for each actor
-- Description: window function ranks films by per‑actor revenue,
--              then picks the best three for every performer.
WITH per_actor_film AS (
    SELECT
           fa."actor_id",
           a."first_name" || ' ' || a."last_name" AS "actor_name",
           fa."film_id",
           f."title",
           SUM(p."amount") / COUNT(DISTINCT fa2."actor_id") AS "actor_revenue"
    FROM   "film_actor" AS fa
    JOIN   "actor"      AS a  ON a."actor_id" = fa."actor_id"
    JOIN   "film"       AS f  ON f."film_id"  = fa."film_id"
    JOIN   "film_actor" AS fa2 ON fa2."film_id" = fa."film_id"
    JOIN   "inventory"  AS i  ON i."film_id"   = fa."film_id"
    JOIN   "rental"     AS r  ON r."inventory_id" = i."inventory_id"
    JOIN   "payment"    AS p  ON p."rental_id"    = r."rental_id"
    GROUP  BY fa."actor_id", fa."film_id"
),
ranked AS (
    SELECT
           *,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY "actor_id"
                              ORDER BY "actor_revenue" DESC) AS rn
    FROM   per_actor_film
)
SELECT DISTINCT
       "actor_id",
       "actor_name",
       "film_id",
       "title",
       ROUND("actor_revenue", 2) AS "revenue_share"
FROM   ranked
WHERE  rn <= 3
ORDER  BY "actor_id", "revenue_share" DESC, "film_id"
LIMIT  20;
```

These queries progressively illuminate how the needed figures are derived,
culminating in the final window‑function query that returns, for every actor,
their three highest‑earning films and the average revenue attributed to them
in each case.
